<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01219</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3853d146-ae3f-439a-aadf-14272a8d0a15</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph</title>
      <para id="para-00001">You can determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, \(y = mx + b\). You can also do the reverse—identify the slope and \(y\)-intercept and use them to find the equation of the line.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Write the linear function shown in the graph.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
        <figure id="fig-00001">
          <media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 7 to 7. A line intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, negative 4), passes through the plotted point (3, negative 2), and intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (4, 0).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_02_04_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para id="para-00004">To find the slope, choose two points on the graph. The \(y\)-intercept is \((0, -4)\), and the graph passes through \((3, -2)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00005"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the slope by counting the rise and run.</para>
      <para id="para-00006">  \(m = \frac {rise}{run}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> \(m = \frac23\)</para>
      <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Find the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
      <para id="para-00009">  \(y\)-intercept \((0,-4)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Substitute the values into \(y=mx+b\).</para>
      <para id="para-00011">  \(y=mx+b\)</para>
      <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> \(y = \frac{2}{3}x=4\)</para>
      <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Step 6 -
  </emphasis>Rewrite the equation using function notation. </para>
      <para id="para-00014">  \(f(x) = \frac{2}{3}x - 4\)</para>
      <title>Try It: Write Linear Functions from a Graph</title>
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00015">Write the linear function shown in the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">
              <figure id="fig-00002">
                <media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 9 to 9. The point (3, negative 1) is plotted. A line intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, 2), intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (9 fourths, 0), and passes through the point (3, negative 1).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_02_04_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00017">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00019">Here is how to write a linear function from a graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00020"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Choose two points from the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">    \((0, 3)\) and \((3, -1)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the slope by counting the rise and run.</para>
            <para id="para-00023">    \(m=\frac{rise}{run}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> \(m=-\frac43\)</para>
            <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Find the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00026">    \((0, 3)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Step 5 - 
  Substitute the values into \(y = mx + b\).</para>
            <para id="para-00028">    \(y = mx + b\)</para>
            <para id="para-00029">
              <emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00030">    \(y=-\frac{4}{3}x+3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Rewrite the equation using function notation.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">    \(f(x)=-\frac{4}{3}x+3\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="d7bebde0-c39c-4cbb-8bc8-e287086de9df" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00033">Which linear function could represent the graph?</para>
            <para dir="ltr" id="para-00034">
              <figure id="fig-00003">
                <media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 7 to 7. A line intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (negative 2, 0), intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, 1) and passes through the plotted point (5, 4).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_02_04_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00035">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00036">\(f(x)=\frac{3}{5}x+1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00037">\(f(x)=\frac{5}{3}x+1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00038">\(f(x)=-\frac{3}{5} x+1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00039">\(f(x)=\frac{3}{5}x-\frac{5}{3}\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00040">Correct! Check yourself: The \(y\)-intercept is \((0, 1)\). Use the points \((5, 4)\) and \((0, 1)\) to find the slope, \(\frac35\). </para>
            <para id="para-00041">Try again. Let’s try again a different way: Notice that the \(y\)-intercept is \((0, 1)\). Use the points \((5, 4)\) and \((0, 1)\) to find the slope.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
